A||||14||Brian84SE||Buick's answer to the fiero?||||1||
Z||000000||Brian84SE||05-22-2000||10:30 AM||||I found out that Buick made a 2 seater car for 2 years from 88-90 or so.  It was called the Regatta.  Anyone know anything about it?||||reg||1
Z||000001||lennyb4||05-22-2000||10:36 AM||||The puick regretta  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"> was supposed to be a challange to the fiero..Thats like putting Earnest in the ring with Mike Tyson. Actually there not bad little cars, my brothers got one..but i like mt fiero alot better (and so does he).||||reg||1
Z||000002||Gold-86SE||05-22-2000||10:44 AM||||If memory serves, which I think it does, it was actually the "reatta" not the regatta.<P>Sorry to sound anal, but if your searching the net spelling counts, well most of the time anyway    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Did a quick search on the net try this page <A HREF="http://www.pixi.com/~jenkins/reatta/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.pixi.com/~jenkins/reatta/</A>  sounded good to me, at first glance.<P>Had to edit, put in the wrong freaking link.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Gold-86SE (edited 05-22-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||007DOUG||05-22-2000||10:59 AM||||The reatta was buicks answer to jaguar and mercedes benz. Especially when made in convertible form (90-91 only) The 88-90 Reattas are really cool. They have a touch screen TV that controls A/C & radio. <P>It was tagged a "two seat personal luxury coupe". <P>It was never a sports car, and buick made no such claim.<P>GM produced alot of specialty cars in the 80's- early 90's:<P>Fiero<BR>Reatta<BR>Allante<P> They plunked a ton of money into design and production, only to give it all up after a short run. Which makes it nice for us collectors.<P>I've been thinking about collecting R's & A's and have been watching their value over the past few years. The Reattas (except for the convertibles) are practically being given away. The Allantes (except the northstars) are holding steady at $10-15K. The N*'s are still 30K at least. No surprise there. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||pollock||05-22-2000||02:16 PM||||I worked on a few Reatta interiors.  I think those cars had the best of everything GM had to offer at the time, in terms of comfort/luxury/trim.  VERY nice cars, especially interior-wise.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by pollock (edited 05-22-2000).]||||reg||14
Z||000005||fiero56||05-22-2000||02:28 PM||||I see Reattas every day. Seemed like they were at every stop light on Saturday. Must have seen 15 that day. I think they look really cool, but they are tons more common than the Fiero. I see very few Fieros here, but see TONS of Reattas. Every time I see a Reatta the placement of the wheels just screams Fiero. From the side a Reatta looks VERY similar to our Fieros. I like them both, but have never driven a Reatta, wouldn't mind buying either.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Monkeyman||05-22-2000||05:42 PM||||Although I was never really impressed with the Reatta, I'd still like to have their digidash with the touch screen controls (like Doug was talking about).  Might be a <B>VERY</B> cool addition to the Fiero (if I could find a place to install them <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">).||||reg||1
Z||000007||DaRkLoRD||05-22-2000||06:54 PM||||The Reatta looks pretty nice.. has a 3.8L V6 too, that'd be cool.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I like the dash too. anyone have a guess as to how much just the dash board (no gauges or instruments..) would cost? If I can't find a good dash to modify for the fiero, I might have to try and make my own..||||reg||1
Z||000008||stsmithgt||05-22-2000||07:19 PM||||My mom has a red Reatta.  Every thing power, leather, and computorized.  The video monitor in the middle of the dash is the control system with a touch screen.  It's bigger than the Fiero. It sits higher off the ground too.  Nice car, but I like my Fiero better.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Cooter||05-22-2000||09:48 PM||||My dad has an 88 red Reatta. Of course it was wrecked when we got it- that was the toughest unibody car I have ever worked on! Very well made- at 150,000 miles it is still going strong. It is a truely unique car, sort of like ours!||||reg||1
Z||000010||87ECO5||05-22-2000||10:41 PM||||I've heard they were hand-built.  Is this true?||||reg||5
Z||000011||DaRkLoRD||05-22-2000||11:21 PM||||I know Ferraris (sp) are, at least some of them, but the Reattas seem too common to be built by hand.. it's not the kind of thing GM does. Of course, I could be wrong and I'm sure I'll be corrected if I am..<P>either way, human or machine built.. they seem like pretty nice cars.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000012||Terrybogin||05-22-2000||11:47 PM||||The owners manual cover states that the Reatta was hand assembled for my driving pleasure. GM did not produce enough Reatta's to cover the costs of the tooling and equipment to build the car. Another orphan because of the bean counters. What a shame.||||reg||1
Z||000013||DJRice||05-23-2000||09:48 AM||||maybe robotic hands...||||reg||1
Z||000014||MikeE||05-24-2000||08:03 PM||||Since the Reatta is nice looking, but common enough that it hasn't achieved serious collector status yet, and comes stock with a 3.8 V6, it might make an interesting recipient for a late model supercharged 3800.  If engine mods were planned it might be better to find a pre-94, pre-OBD II 3800 SC even though it has less power stock.  On the other hand, a northstar would fit nicely although it would be a bit more work and expense.  Any thoughts?<p>[This message has been edited by MikeE (edited 05-24-2000).]||||reg||3
